before:
If equals returns true, the two objects must have the same hashCode. Unequal objects may still collide; a hash code is not a unique identifier. Records generate compatible equals and hashCode implementations.

hashCode() takes no arguments and returns an int hash. After the Key record declaration shown below, this checks the equal-value requirement without assuming any particular hash number:
```java
Key first = new Key(3);
Key second = new Key(3);
System.out.println(first.hashCode() == second.hashCode());
```
Output: true.

Complete local Java example; the app itself does not execute it:
```java
import java.util.HashSet;
record Key(int id) {}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Key a = new Key(7);
        Key b = new Key(7);
        HashSet<Key> keys = new HashSet<>();
        keys.add(a);
        keys.add(b);
        System.out.println(a == b);
        System.out.println(a.equals(b));
        System.out.println(keys.size());
    }
}

```

after:
Compare object identity, data equality, and the set's member count.

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
record Key(int id) {}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Key a = new Key(7);
        Key b = new Key(7);
        HashSet<Key> keys = new HashSet<>();
        keys.add(a);
        keys.add(b);
        System.out.println(a == b);
        System.out.println(a.equals(b));
        System.out.println(keys.size());
    }
}

```
